        <div id="wh-and-w" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is the Difference Between Watt-Hours and Watts?
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Watt-hours (Wh)</strong> are the size of the tank: how much energy the station stores. <strong>Watts (W)</strong> are the width of the pipe: how fast the inverter can hand that energy out. Mixing the two up is the costliest mistake buyers make, because a station with a big inverter and a small battery will start your refrigerator and then run flat within a few hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why check output first?</h3>

          <p>
            If an appliance's running draw, or its starting surge, goes past the station's continuous or surge rating, the inverter cuts out. A refrigerator may run on 100 to 200 watts yet need 1,000 watts or more for a split second when the compressor kicks on. That is why a 300-watt station cannot run a fridge however full its battery is, while a 1,500-watt unit can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you turn capacity into hours?</h3>

          <p>
            Divide the station's watt-hours by the load's average draw in watts, then take off 10 to 15 percent for inverter losses. On paper, a 1,024Wh station feeding a 100-watt average load lasts about 9 hours. The word average does a lot of work here: a refrigerator switches on and off, so its average sits far below its running draw, which is how a 1kWh station can carry one for most of a day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do boost modes add real power?</h3>

          <p>
            Not in the way the names suggest. Modes such as X-Boost or Power Lifting run resistive loads like kettles and hair dryers by dropping the voltage, so the appliance runs slower or cooler. They will not start a motor that exceeds the station's true surge rating, and they add nothing to capacity. Trust the continuous watt rating.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE QUICK FILTER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">To run a refrigerator, start at 1,000Wh and 1,500W.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Smaller stations are for phones, lights, routers, fans and CPAPs. Bigger ones buy more hours rather than more capability, until the 2kWh class, which can keep a fridge and a freezer going for a full day.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

