          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much power does a refrigerator really use?</h3>

          <p>
            Fridges get the closest look because they are why most people buy a station. A modern full-size model uses roughly 1 to 2 kWh a day, depending on its size and age, the room temperature and how often the door opens. Keep the door shut and the thermostat at a safe 37 to 40 degrees and the average draw falls. An older fridge, or a second one in a hot garage, can use a great deal more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can a power station handle heating, cooling or cooking?</h3>

          <p>
            Mostly not. A 1,500-watt space heater would empty a 1kWh station in well under an hour, and a window air conditioner would do it in a couple of hours. Electric resistance cooking behaves much the same. If those loads matter during your outages, you need a generator, solar feeding a large station, or both. A gas furnace blower is a realistic exception: it pulls a few hundred watts while running, which a 2kWh station can support for a number of hours of cycling.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What changes CPAP runtime?</h3>

          <p>
            The humidifier. Heated humidification can double or triple the draw. Many CPAP makers sell DC cables that skip the station's inverter and stretch runtime noticeably. Ask your equipment supplier what your particular machine draws at your pressure setting.
          </p>
