          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How long does an LFP station last?</h3>

          <p>
            A rating of 3,000 cycles means you could run the station from full to empty every day for eight years before it drops to about 80 percent capacity, and even heavy users tend to retire a unit for other reasons first. NMC stations are fine for occasional camping, but use one as a daily solar buffer and the cycle count will catch up with you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is LFP safer indoors?</h3>

          <p>
            It gives a real margin. LFP cells are far less prone to thermal runaway, the chain reaction behind lithium battery fires. That does not make it acceptable to bury a station under blankets or leave it in a hot car, but it matters for a device that lives in your home. Look for UL listings or equivalent certifications on the station itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What counts as a cycle?</h3>

          <p>
            Manufacturers count one cycle as one full discharge equivalent, so using half the battery twice adds up to a single cycle. Most outage owners use a station a few dozen times a year, which means an LFP unit will probably outlast its electronics, its warranty and likely the port standard it ships with. For that kind of use, chemistry is really a choice about safety and weight, and LFP wins on safety.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every lithium battery ages with time as well as with cycles, and neither chemistry likes sitting at 100 percent charge in a hot garage. That is why the storage advice in section 11 matters more than the badge on the box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="charging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Recharge a Power Station When the Power Is Out?
          </h2>

          <p>
            With solar panels or a small inverter generator running outdoors; a car socket helps only a little. In a long outage, a station is only as useful as your plan for refilling it. When the grid is up, wall charging is the fastest option: current 1kWh LFP units commonly reach 80 percent in under an hour, which pays off during rolling blackouts and when a generator runs in short bursts.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
