          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who should buy which?</h3>

          <p>
            A station alone suits short outages, apartments and condos, and households that mainly need to protect a CPAP, a fridge and the internet. A generator suits outages that last days, homes that need heat, a well pump or air conditioning, and any house with a sump pump that has to keep running. Get both if you want silent nights and long-outage resilience; it is the most comfortable setup covered on this site.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator rankings</a> cover the fuel-powered side, including transfer switches and safe placement, and our <a href="/best-gas-generator/">gas generator guide</a> covers fuel storage and run times.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How Should You Store a Power Station, and How Long Does It Last?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Store it indoors at about 50 to 80 percent charge and moderate temperature, topping it up every three months or so, and an LFP unit is rated for thousands of cycles. Power stations are among the safest backup options, but they are still large lithium batteries, so a few rules apply.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where should it sit?</h3>

          <p>
            On a hard surface with the vents clear, out of direct sun, away from water and away from anything that could fall onto it. Never charge or run it while covered, and use the charger and cables the manufacturer supplies or specifies.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What charge level is best for storage?</h3>

          <p>
            Partial. Most manufacturers recommend roughly 50 to 80 percent for long storage at moderate indoor temperatures, with a top-up every three months or so. Sitting full in a hot garage ages the cells faster than anything else. Before storm season, charge it to full, and leave it plugged in only if the manufacturer supports continuous charging or UPS mode.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you charge it in the cold?</h3>

          <p>
            Not below freezing, unless the station has built-in battery heating. That applies to lithium cells generally, LFP included, and many units simply refuse to charge in the cold to protect themselves. If yours lives in an unheated shed or vehicle, bring it inside to warm up before charging.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What are the warning signs of a failing battery?</h3>

          <p>
            A swollen case, an unusual smell, hissing, or heat while idle. Any of those means stop using it, move it outdoors away from buildings if you can do so safely, and contact the manufacturer. When a unit reaches the end of its life, take it to a battery recycler rather than the trash; most manufacturers and many home centers accept them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Portable Power Stations
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping on watt-hours alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A large battery behind a small inverter still cannot start a refrigerator or a pump. Confirm that continuous output covers your biggest load's running draw and that the surge rating covers its startup, then check capacity for hours. Both numbers must fit, and the continuous rating, not a boost mode, is the one to rely on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting on it to run a space heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Resistance heaters, electric kettles and hair dryers pull 1,200 to 1,500 watts, enough to drain a 1kWh station in well under an hour. For heat during outages, a gas furnace blower is a realistic load for a station. A space heater is not, unless you also have a generator or a very large battery bank.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing the solar panel's rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 200-watt portable panel seldom produces 200 watts. Angle, heat, haze and season commonly cut real output to half the rating or less. Build your solar plan around a cautious daily figure, buy more panel than the math says you need, and move the panels several times a day to track the sun.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it full in a hot garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium cells age fastest when they sit at full charge in high heat, and a station left at 100 percent through a summer in the garage loses capacity permanently. Keep it indoors at roughly half to 80 percent, top it up every few months, and charge it to full only ahead of storm season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the car in the garage to recharge it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some owners try charging a station from a running vehicle parked in an attached garage. That fills the space with the same lethal carbon monoxide a generator would. Charge from the car only while driving, or with the vehicle fully outdoors and away from openings, or use the wall, solar or an outdoor generator.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 1,000Wh power station run a refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually about 6 to 14 hours. Most modern full-size refrigerators average 60 to 150 watts across a day because the compressor cycles on and off, and inverter losses take a share. Older, bigger or garage fridges use more. Keep the door shut and power the fridge in blocks of an hour or two to stretch the battery over a longer outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a portable power station indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A power station has no engine and no exhaust, so it poses no carbon monoxide risk, which is its biggest advantage over a generator. Set it on a hard surface with the vents clear, away from heat, water and anything flammable, and never cover it while it charges or runs. A fuel generator used to recharge it must stay outdoors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station run a CPAP all night?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In most cases, yes. Without the heated humidifier, most CPAPs draw 30 to 60 watts, so even a 250Wh station can get through a night and a 1,000Wh unit can cover several. Heated humidification and high pressure settings push the draw up sharply. A manufacturer DC cable avoids inverter losses; confirm your machine's real draw with your equipment supplier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station power a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Even large expandable systems in the 3 to 6 kWh range cover essentials for a day or two, not central air, electric heat or an electric range. Whole-house battery backup means a permanently installed home battery system put in by professionals. For whole-house coverage at lower cost, a standby or large portable generator is still the usual answer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to recharge a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a 1kWh LFP station, about an hour from a wall outlet in fast mode, with many reaching 80 percent in under an hour. A small inverter generator typically takes one to two hours, a 200-watt panel four to eight hours in good sun, and a 12V car socket ten hours or more. Solar output falls with clouds, heat and winter sun.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many solar panels do you need to charge a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expect real output of about half the panel rating in decent sun. Refilling a 1,000Wh station in a single day takes roughly 200 to 400 watts of panels in summer, and more in winter. Stay inside the station's solar input voltage and wattage limits, which its spec sheet lists, and wire the panels to match those limits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station run a microwave or a space heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A microwave, yes, on a 1kWh-class station with enough output: the 1,800W DELTA 2 runs one, and the 1,500W Jackery Explorer 1000 v2 can run one on its own. The 300W RIVER 3 cannot. A space heater is a poor fit, because a 1,500-watt heater empties a 1kWh station in well under an hour.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

